{"name":"Green Pear","hex":"#79be58","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"pear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible fruit produced by the pear tree, similar to an apple but typically elongated towards the stem.","A type of fruit tree (Pyrus communis)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pear"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pear","blocks":[{"text":"Pears are a genus of trees, Pyrus, in the family Rosaceae, that bear an edible apple-like fruit of the same name. Several species of pears are cultivated for their fruit and juices, while others are grown as ornamental flowering trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pears.jpg/330px-Pears.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pear","wikidata":"Q434"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Shades of yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color yellow may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a yellow or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Color_icon_yellow.svg/330px-Color_icon_yellow.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_yellow","wikidata":"Q7460348"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pear_DS.jpg/500px-Pear_DS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pear_DS.jpg/1280px-Pear_DS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Pear_DS.jpg/500px-Pear_DS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APear_DS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Pear_tree_in_Hamedan_Iran.jpg/250px-Pear_tree_in_Hamedan_Iran.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Pear_tree_in_Hamedan_Iran.jpg/330px-Pear_tree_in_Hamedan_Ira